56
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:26,080
OK.

57
00:06:33,010 --> 00:06:35,610
So why is this
relevant here for us?

58
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:40,170
So what we were
looking at last time--

59
00:06:40,170 --> 00:06:43,050
and I'm only going
to use the limsup.

60
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:46,630
So last time, we had a sequence.

61
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:57,060
So a-n was a sequence,
and we defined

62
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:59,500
what we call a power series.

63
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:02,860
And again, power
series, think about that

64
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:06,260
x as being just
some fixed number,

65
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:07,900
and you have the sequence a-n.

66
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:14,740
X is some fixed number, and then
you form the sequence a-n x-n,

67
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:16,980
right?

68
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:23,180
And so for each x fixed,
this gives you some number.

69
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:24,860
Maybe, if it's converged.

70
00:07:24,860 --> 00:07:26,360
I mean, you can ask for each x.

71
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,420
You can ask, does this
sequence converge or not?

72
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:32,260
And this here is
called a power series.

73
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:34,760
So you're summing
from 0 to whatever.

74
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:36,900
Or you're starting from
one, or whatever you're

75
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:38,840
starting with up to infinity.

76
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,700
So this is a power series.

77
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:48,840
And so now for power
series, the question

78
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:54,880
is, how serious is
the question is,

79
00:07:54,880 --> 00:08:01,510
for which, for which
x does it converge?

80
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:10,800
So that's what we're
interested in,.

81
00:08:13,963 --> 00:08:15,880
So just let me remind
you, because we're going

82
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:18,580
to use that anyway in a minute.

83
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:24,280
So example from last time.

84
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:34,600
The example we looked at last
time was we're looking at x-n.

85
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,840
And we looked at some
other examples, too,

86
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:39,880
but this was the one
we're focused on.

87
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:42,020
So this here is a power series.

88
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:47,750
The a-n in this case here
are 1 over n factorial.

89
00:08:53,810 --> 00:09:00,250
And for each fixed
x, so for x fixed

90
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:03,770
we looked at whether or not
this sequence converges.

91
00:09:03,770 --> 00:09:07,730
And so we think about
this thing here.

92
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:08,950
We thought this here.

93
00:09:08,950 --> 00:09:11,410
We thought about it
as just a sequence

94
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:16,270
like this, sum of a sequence.

95
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:21,890
So this is as a series like
this, where the d-n here is now

96
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:24,790
x to the n over n factorial.

97
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:32,290
And so in order to determine for
which x it converges-- so just

98
00:09:32,290 --> 00:09:33,870
think about x as being fixed.

99
00:09:33,870 --> 00:09:35,300
We use the ratio test.

100
00:09:41,290 --> 00:09:46,670
So we use the ratio
test, and the ratio test

101
00:09:46,670 --> 00:09:52,270
said that if you're looking
at d-n plus 1 over d-n,

102
00:09:52,270 --> 00:09:58,470
this here is where d-n is
just x to the n plus 1,

103
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:07,430
over n plus 1 factorial, divided
by x to the n over n factorial.

104
00:10:07,430 --> 00:10:09,350
And that's the same.

105
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:13,290
So when you divide by a
fraction, you just switch.

106
00:10:13,290 --> 00:10:17,230
So this is the same as x
to the n plus 1, divided

107
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:25,310
by n plus 1 factorial, times
n factorial, times x to the n.

108
00:10:25,310 --> 00:10:27,710
And you just think
about this, the top

109
00:10:27,710 --> 00:10:32,710
here, as x to the n times
x, times n factorial.

110
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:37,110
And the denominator you think
about as n plus 1 times n

111
00:10:37,110 --> 00:10:39,310
factorial, times x to the n.

112
00:10:39,310 --> 00:10:44,070
And you see that this here and
this here cancel with those two.

113
00:10:44,070 --> 00:10:50,160
And so you get x over n plus 1.

114
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:55,920
And this here converges to 0,
so therefore the power series

115
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:58,160
is convergent for all x.

116
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:06,400
So that was a particular
example of a power series,

117
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:11,720
and it worked well
with the ratio test.

118
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:13,980
But the word test
actually is better.

119
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:18,940
And this was related to a point
somebody raised last time.

120
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:23,740
And that point was
for the ratio test,

121
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:28,280
it was implicitly somehow in
the ratio test, was something

122
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:32,160
about that from some
stage on, the next element

123
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:34,380
is smaller than
the previous one.

124
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:38,520
And you don't really
need to have that.

125
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:43,440
I mean, you certainly don't need
it for a series to converge,

126
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:49,260
but it's not even necessary
if you apply the right test.

127
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:56,100
And so this brings
us to what's called

128
00:11:56,100 --> 00:12:00,460
the radius of convergence.

129
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:08,620
So this is a crucial concept,
and by this crucial concept,

130
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:12,140
because you see a priori
what you could imagine--

131
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:18,200
so suppose you just write
down a power series like this.

132
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:28,060
Maybe it's convergent
for when x is equal to 5,

133
00:12:28,060 --> 00:12:30,980
it's divergent when
x is equal to 10,

134
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:35,440
and maybe it's converging
again if x is equal to 20.

135
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:38,500
That's not actually
going to be the case.

136
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:41,320
So there's something called
radius of convergence.

137
00:12:50,990 --> 00:12:53,190
And the thing is
that what you get out

138
00:12:53,190 --> 00:12:57,750
of this radius of convergence
is that a power series like this

139
00:12:57,750 --> 00:13:02,830
is convergent on some interval,
and then outside this interval

140
00:13:02,830 --> 00:13:04,670
is divergent.

141
00:13:04,670 --> 00:13:08,950
So it's not like you have
some that is convergent here,

142
00:13:08,950 --> 00:13:12,550
it's divergent there, and
then it's converging again.

143
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:14,090
That's not what's
going to happen.

144
00:13:14,090 --> 00:13:16,590
OK?

145
00:13:16,590 --> 00:13:20,990
Part of this we already
saw in the different tests

146
00:13:20,990 --> 00:13:27,830
that at the boundary
of this interval,

147
00:13:27,830 --> 00:13:29,750
the test is
inconclusive, so you have

148
00:13:29,750 --> 00:13:33,950
to check the
boundaries separately.

149
00:13:33,950 --> 00:13:38,790
And so the idea is
that you check--

150
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:45,370
so you have a power
series like this,

151
00:13:45,370 --> 00:13:48,810
and you think about-- again,
you're thinking about d-n

152
00:13:48,810 --> 00:13:51,022
as x to the n.

153
00:13:51,022 --> 00:13:54,490
X, again, is thought
of being fixed,

154
00:13:54,490 --> 00:13:56,750
and now you apply the root test.

155
00:13:56,750 --> 00:13:58,530
So apply the root
test, but I'm going

156
00:13:58,530 --> 00:14:03,000
to apply it in a better form
than what we did last time.

157
00:14:07,050 --> 00:14:10,300
Maybe I'll just state first the
better form of the root test.

158
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:21,530
Here's the real version
of the root test.

159
00:14:21,530 --> 00:14:25,050
So suppose you take a series.

160
00:14:25,050 --> 00:14:26,990
Suppose you have
some series here.

161
00:14:30,364 --> 00:14:34,330
Let's just call it d.

162
00:14:34,330 --> 00:14:36,040
Suppose you take a
series like this.

163
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:45,900
So now the root test,
you took d-n, you

164
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:49,270
took the absolute value,
and you took the n-th root.

165
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:55,500
When I looked at the
root test last time,

166
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:59,700
we looked at whether this
is convergent or not.

167
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:03,010
So we said if it was
converging to something, right?

168
00:15:03,010 --> 00:15:06,740
but you could form
this thing here.

169
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:09,480
So you could form
from this here.

170
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:10,520
You could form this.

171
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:14,560
This is now a sequence, and you
could look at limsup of this.

172
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:25,340
These here are all
non-negative numbers,

173
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:30,740
and this limsup here is now
going to be either infinite

174
00:15:30,740 --> 00:15:34,260
or it's going to be some
real non-negative number.

175
00:15:34,260 --> 00:15:37,740
This here is going
to be infinite.

176
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:40,440
Usually, we don't talk about
that it's converging if it's

177
00:15:40,440 --> 00:15:43,850
infinite or it's some number.

178
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:50,040
Let's call it d, I guess.

179
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:52,800
OK?

180
00:15:52,800 --> 00:16:01,260
And the thing is that if you
said r equal to 1 over d--

181
00:16:04,270 --> 00:16:04,770
sorry.

182
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:12,400
This is just a root test, the
adult version of the root test,

183
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:15,540
because this applies
to all sequences.

184
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:18,040
For any sequence, you have this.

185
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:22,420
For any sequence d-n, if
you're looking at this series,

186
00:16:22,420 --> 00:16:27,840
you can look at this, and
it's either infinity or d.

187
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:32,240
And then if d here
is less than 1,

188
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:34,180
then the series is convergent.

189
00:16:40,690 --> 00:16:45,390
If d is bigger than 1,
the series is divergent.

190
00:16:51,970 --> 00:16:55,510
And if d is equal to
1, it's inconclusive.

191
00:17:02,610 --> 00:17:07,210
The difference between this and
what we talked about last time

192
00:17:07,210 --> 00:17:12,810
was that last time we assumed
that this thing here converged.

193
00:17:12,810 --> 00:17:17,170
Here, I'm not assuming it, but
then I'm looking at the limsup

194
00:17:17,170 --> 00:17:18,690
instead.

195
00:17:18,690 --> 00:17:24,810
So this does give us a
number, and it's just

196
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:29,530
a matter of whether this
limsup is smaller than 1.

197
00:17:29,530 --> 00:17:31,990
And then it's convergent
or bigger than 1,

198
00:17:31,990 --> 00:17:33,110
then it's divergent.

199
00:17:36,830 --> 00:17:39,430
Now I'll come back to
the proof, but the proof

200
00:17:39,430 --> 00:17:41,970
is really kind of the
same as the one we gave.

201
00:17:41,970 --> 00:17:45,790
The one we gave,
really, if you mimic

202
00:17:45,790 --> 00:17:50,430
what we did in the
special case last time,

203
00:17:50,430 --> 00:17:54,030
then the proof would
still apply to this case

204
00:17:54,030 --> 00:18:01,110
here and give this
result. But let's

205
00:18:01,110 --> 00:18:06,130
take this more fancy
version of the root test.

206
00:18:06,130 --> 00:18:07,740
Let's take that for granted.

207
00:18:15,870 --> 00:18:20,870
So now I have a power
series like this.

208
00:18:20,870 --> 00:18:31,750
Just for clarity, I define
d-n to be just a-n, times x-n.

209
00:18:31,750 --> 00:18:42,880
And I'm interested in is
for what x, for what x

210
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:44,380
does the power series converge?

211
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:07,560
So you are looking at limsup
of the n-th root of d-n

212
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:16,155
is a-n times x-n, and you take
the absolute value of that.

213
00:19:16,155 --> 00:19:18,640
That's what you're
looking at, and you're

214
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:23,120
asking is this smaller
than 1 or bigger than 1?

215
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:27,997
But this thing here, right, this
is the same as limsup, right?

216
00:19:27,997 --> 00:19:29,580
If you're looking
at this thing here--

217
00:19:29,580 --> 00:19:32,100
let's do a little side
calculation-- if you're

218
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:38,860
looking at the n-th
root of a-n times x-n,

219
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:44,780
this thing here is the same as
the n-th root of this product

220
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:45,280
here.

221
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:49,220
Absolute value is the
same as this times

222
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:53,500
x absolute value to n.

223
00:19:53,500 --> 00:19:55,820
And when you take
the n-th root, you

224
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:59,820
may as well take the n-th root
of each of those two factors

225
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:01,460
individually.

226
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:02,920
So this is what you get.

227
00:20:08,020 --> 00:20:11,500
But this thing here, you
see, this thing here,

228
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:15,380
the n-th root-- this
is whatever it is.

229
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:18,020
Can't do much about that.

230
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:20,740
But this thing here,
that's just the norm

231
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:24,540
of x, and the norm of x has the
property that when you raise it

232
00:20:24,540 --> 00:20:26,580
to the n-th power,
you're getting this.

233
00:20:26,580 --> 00:20:29,670
So this is the n.

234
00:20:29,670 --> 00:20:32,310
So this means that
this thing here

235
00:20:32,310 --> 00:20:41,920
is the same as the norm of x
limsup of the n-th root of a-n.

236
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:47,570
Often you do it like this.

237
00:20:47,570 --> 00:20:49,130
You don't have to do this.

238
00:20:49,130 --> 00:20:52,850
It's usually clear anyway, but
often you write it like that.

239
00:20:55,456 --> 00:21:00,630
So now what you do
is you are asking

240
00:21:00,630 --> 00:21:03,890
when is this number
here smaller than 1?

